{ This move usually signals that White intends to sidestep the Open Sicilian. }
2... g6
{ I recommend 2...d6 in my Najdorf course and that was an extensive body of work that I am quite proud of. You can definitely check that out and I believe it would cover a Dragon player as well as it covers the Najdorf player. However, now that we are aiming for the Dragon and have more freedom when it comes to move orders, I decided to propose a rare, but very efficient move order for us: 2...g6! . I believe it is the perfect weapon for the Dragon player. We get a much better version than 2...d6 of the Grand Prix attack ( 3.f4 ) and more flexibility in the closed Sicilian ( 3.g3 ). rnbqkbnr/pp1ppp1p/6p1/2p5/4P3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKBNR w KQkq - 0 3 3.Nf3 and 3.Nge2 will transpose to the Dragon after a clever move order trickery that I will teach you and 3.d4 is the only critical option, but that works out quite well for Black as well, as we shall see. One other thing I should mention is that 2...Nc6 is also an excellent option, especially if you are happy with the Accelerated Dragon after 3.Nf3 g6 4.d4 cxd4 5.Nxd4 Bg7 etc, but for our repertoire, I feel rnbqkbnr/pp1ppppp/8/2p5/4P3/2N5/PPPP1PPP/R1BQKBNR b KQkq - 1 2 2...g6 is more apt and is also much more likely to take your opponent by surprise, which could confuse him and lure him into lines he doesn't want to be in. }
