) ( 5. Ld3 Sc6 6. Sxc6 bxc6 ) 5... g6
{ The move that defines the Dragon opening, as our pawn structure is now supposed to resemble a dragon. An important characteristic of this setup is that we have basically no weaknesses (the e7-pawn at the base of the chain barely counts), but it does create a target for a kingside attack and White's most principled approach of castling long does ask Black some important questions. Therefore, I believe the Yugoslav attack lines, in which White castles long, are quite important to know move by move, while slower setups (with the short castle) can be played 'by ear'. Though to get that 'ear' tuned right, it is still good to go over all the variations in great detail and study some model games, but when it comes to memorizing a lot of concrete lines, the long castle beats the short castle hands down. }
6. Le3
{ By far the most principled move, going for the so-called Yugoslav Attack. True to the nature of the position, White wants to quickly castle long and start an attack on the kingside. I believe you will encounter this in most of your games, no matter what your level is. Both the amateurs and the professionals are in sync here and most of them take up the challenge and aim for the long castle plans. And rightly so. The following lines will give you a good taste of what awaits you in the main chapters, but so as to be fully prepared, I certainly recommend going through the main body of this course, as it was hard to fit everything into this Quickstarter Guide. }
( 6. Lc4
{ This is similar to theClassical system with 6. Be2. White just wants to complete his kingside development and castle short. Not very ambitious, nor dangerous. }
